WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.119
Skills are what you build when you want a procedure

2
00:00:03.119 --> 00:00:06.699
Claude pulls in automatically whenever it fits a request,

3
00:00:06.699 --> 00:00:09.720
without someone having to remember to type a command.

4
00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:11.119
Over the next few minutes,

5
00:00:11.119 --> 00:00:14.680
we'll look at how skills differ from slash commands and sub-agents,

6
00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:17.139
the decision framework for choosing to build one,

7
00:00:17.139 --> 00:00:20.879
and where skills sit in the broader instruction stack.

8
00:00:20.879 --> 00:00:24.000
Let's dive in. Claude code gives you

9
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.120
more than one way to package a repeatable instruction,

10
00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:30.719
and picking the wrong one is how workflows get over-engineered.

11
00:00:30.719 --> 00:00:33.580
A skill is a reusable instruction package,

12
00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:38.099
a skill.md file plus any optional supporting files.

13
00:00:38.099 --> 00:00:39.900
Claude loads it automatically when

14
00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:42.279
its description matches what you're asking for,

15
00:00:42.279 --> 00:00:44.479
or you invoke it directly by name.

16
00:00:44.479 --> 00:00:47.720
A slash command is really a subset of skills now,

17
00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:50.439
created in the.claude commands directory,

18
00:00:50.439 --> 00:00:52.479
and invoked with a slash name.

19
00:00:52.479 --> 00:00:54.259
These aren't separate mechanisms,

20
00:00:54.259 --> 00:00:56.200
only different loading patterns.

21
00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:00.479
A manual-only command sets a disable model invocation flag,

22
00:01:00.479 --> 00:01:02.860
so Claude won't fire it on its own,

23
00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:06.160
which fits a shortcut you want triggered by hand.

24
00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:09.639
A skill can also load itself when its description matches.

25
00:01:09.639 --> 00:01:14.839
A sub-agent is a delegated autonomous worker with its own context and tools,

26
00:01:14.839 --> 00:01:18.440
an active process rather than a passive template.

27
00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:22.599
Skills occupy a specific position in the instruction stack.

28
00:01:22.599 --> 00:01:25.319
At the bottom, claude.md holds

29
00:01:25.319 --> 00:01:30.000
always applied facts and rules that Claude sees on every interaction.

30
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.739
No matching or invocation involved.

31
00:01:32.739 --> 00:01:36.199
In the middle, skills are description-gated procedures.

32
00:01:36.199 --> 00:01:39.160
Claude reads each skill's description and pulls

33
00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:43.000
the skill in only when it's relevant to what you asked.

34
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:46.400
At the top, sub-agents are autonomous workers.

35
00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:50.400
You spin up when a task should be completed independently.

36
00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:53.639
Choosing the right layer prevents over-engineering.

37
00:01:53.839 --> 00:01:58.919
A simple always-on rule buried in a skill adds complexity for nothing,

38
00:01:58.919 --> 00:02:02.839
and a frequently needed team pattern marked manual-only

39
00:02:02.839 --> 00:02:06.519
means everyone has to remember to invoke it by hand.

40
00:02:06.519 --> 00:02:10.080
Match the mechanism to how it should load.

41
00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:13.600
Four patterns tell you a skill is the right choice.

42
00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:17.600
Recurring multi-step workflows that you run across many sessions

43
00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:23.080
or across a team benefit from being captured once in skill.md

44
00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:25.559
rather than redescribed each time.

45
00:02:25.559 --> 00:02:30.320
Team-wide conventions that should apply consistently for every contributor

46
00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:35.679
belong in a shared skill, not in each person's individual claude.md.

47
00:02:35.679 --> 00:02:39.679
Procedures that should load automatically when relevant,

48
00:02:39.679 --> 00:02:44.880
not on a typed command, fit the way skills get matched by their description.

49
00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:48.399
And reusable instruction packages that should run the same way

50
00:02:48.399 --> 00:02:52.720
every time benefit from the structure a skill.md gives you.

51
00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:58.399
If your workflow fits two or more of these patterns, it belongs in a skill.

52
00:02:58.399 --> 00:03:01.679
Knowing when not to build a skill is just as important.

53
00:03:01.679 --> 00:03:07.279
One-time tasks don't justify the overhead of writing a formal skill.md.

54
00:03:07.279 --> 00:03:09.600
Skills exist for reuse.

55
00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:13.320
A task you'll run once is better handled in line.

56
00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:16.639
Simple lookups don't need a separate skill at all.

57
00:03:16.639 --> 00:03:20.240
A claude.md note or a quick slash command

58
00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:22.880
is less friction for straightforward requests,

59
00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:28.360
and some patterns are better handled by a claude.md rule that's always on

60
00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:32.559
rather than a skill that only loads when it matches a request.

61
00:03:32.559 --> 00:03:35.639
If the instruction should apply everywhere unconditionally,

62
00:03:35.639 --> 00:03:38.360
claude.md is the right place.

63
00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:40.279
Skills add complexity.

64
00:03:40.279 --> 00:03:45.479
Only build one when the complexity is justified by real reuse value.

65
00:03:45.479 --> 00:03:48.360
The build decision framework comes down to three factors,

66
00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:52.080
frequency, complexity, and reusability.

67
00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:54.880
Frequency asks how often this workflow appears

68
00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:57.080
across sessions or team members.

69
00:03:57.080 --> 00:04:00.839
If it's rare, a formal skill may not be worth the investment.

70
00:04:00.839 --> 00:04:04.399
Complexity asks whether the workflow is multi-step enough

71
00:04:04.399 --> 00:04:09.559
that a reusable template provides meaningful value over describing it in line.

72
00:04:09.559 --> 00:04:12.360
Simple one-liners don't justify the overhead.

73
00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:16.880
Reusability asks whether more than one person, session, or team context

74
00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:19.880
will benefit from the skill running consistently.

75
00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:23.959
A workflow that scores high on all three is a strong skill candidate.

76
00:04:23.959 --> 00:04:27.200
One that scores low on even one factor should make you consider

77
00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:30.720
whether a slash command or a claude.md rule

78
00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:33.519
handles it better with less friction.

79
00:04:33.519 --> 00:04:38.399
Skills and subagents are both powerful, but they solve different problems.

80
00:04:38.399 --> 00:04:43.640
A skill is a passive instruction package stored in skill.md.

81
00:04:43.679 --> 00:04:45.959
When its description matches the request,

82
00:04:45.959 --> 00:04:49.920
Claude code loads it and follows the instructions itself.

83
00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:51.679
The skill is the recipe.

84
00:04:51.679 --> 00:04:53.559
Claude code is the cook.

85
00:04:53.559 --> 00:04:58.040
A subagent is an active autonomous worker you delegate a task to.

86
00:04:58.040 --> 00:05:01.799
It has its own context window, tools, and scope.

87
00:05:01.799 --> 00:05:05.399
It runs independently and reports back when done.

88
00:05:05.399 --> 00:05:09.239
You'll run into scenarios where you have to choose between these two.

89
00:05:09.239 --> 00:05:12.359
The key question is whether you need an instruction package that

90
00:05:12.359 --> 00:05:15.200
loads when it matches a request, or whether you

91
00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:19.359
need a worker to handle something autonomously in parallel.

92
00:05:19.359 --> 00:05:20.880
Packages versus workers.

93
00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:24.040
Skills versus subagents.

94
00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:27.079
Skills are worth reaching for when a procedure should show up

95
00:05:27.079 --> 00:05:30.399
on its own at the moment it fits, without anyone

96
00:05:30.399 --> 00:05:32.519
having to remember to ask for it.

97
00:05:32.519 --> 00:05:36.279
That is the whole value of a description-gated middle layer.

98
00:05:36.279 --> 00:05:39.640
And it's why frequency, complexity, and reusability

99
00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:42.959
are the questions to ask before you write a skill.

100
00:05:42.959 --> 00:05:47.239
If a workflow only comes up once, or if it should apply unconditionally

101
00:05:47.239 --> 00:05:52.000
everywhere, a simpler mechanism might serve you better.

102
00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:53.480
That wraps up this lesson.

103
00:05:53.480 --> 00:05:55.839
I'll see you in the next one.

